      "worked": "Let $u=120^{\\circ}-\\frac{2\\theta}{3}$. The modified domain is $-120^{\\circ}\\le u\\le120^{\\circ}$.\n\nThe related angle is $30^{\\circ}$. Cosine is positive in quadrants I and IV, giving both $-30^{\\circ}$ and $30^{\\circ}$ in this domain.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos u&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\\\u&=-30^{\\circ},\\ 30^{\\circ}\\\\120^{\\circ}-\\frac{2\\theta}{3}&=-30^{\\circ}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad120^{\\circ}-\\frac{2\\theta}{3}=30^{\\circ}\\\\\\frac{2\\theta}{3}&=150^{\\circ}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\frac{2\\theta}{3}=90^{\\circ}\\\\\\theta&=225^{\\circ}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\theta=135^{\\circ}\\end{align*}$$\n\nBoth values lie in the original domain. Hence $\\theta=135^{\\circ},\\ 225^{\\circ}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
